  <h2>The Classic: 20 RMB Viewpoint, Xingping</h2>
  <p>The shot everyone wants. A 15-minute walk along the river from Xingping old street. The viewing platform is marked — you'll see other photographers. <strong>Time:</strong> 6:30-8:00 AM for mist on the water and soft light. A calm day gives you perfect reflections of the karst peaks in the river. <strong>Lens:</strong> 24-70mm. Tripod recommended for misty mornings and lower light.</p>

  <h2>Li River Bend: Laozhai Mountain, Xingping</h2>
  <p>The view that graces photography magazines. A 40-50 minute steep climb from Xingping (1,159 steps). The summit overlooks a horseshoe bend in the Li River with peaks receding in layers. <strong>Time:</strong> Sunrise (start climbing 45-60 minutes before). <strong>Lens:</strong> Wide angle (16-35mm) for the full bend, or a telephoto (70-200mm) to compress the layers of peaks. Bring a headlamp for the pre-dawn climb.</p>

  <h2>Yulong River with Bamboo Rafts: Gongnong Bridge</h2>
  <p>The Ten Mile Gallery road crosses the Yulong River here. From the bridge, you can photograph bamboo rafts passing underneath with karst peaks in the background. <strong>Time:</strong> 7-9 AM when the morning light is soft and the rafts are spaced apart. <strong>Lens:</strong> 70-200mm for compression. A polarizing filter helps cut glare off the water.</p>

  <h2>Moon Hill Summit Arch</h2>
  <p>Photograph the landscape through the natural stone arch. Both the arch itself and the panorama from the upper viewpoint are worth shooting. <strong>Time:</strong> Late afternoon — the sun sets behind the peaks to the west, casting golden light. <strong>Lens:</strong> Wide angle (16-35mm) for the arch, telephoto for compressed peak layers.</p>

  <h2>Longji Rice Terraces Sunrise: Ping'an</h2>
  <p>The Nine Dragons and Five Tigers viewpoint in Ping'an village. Framed by terraced rice paddies that flood with water in spring, reflecting the sky. <strong>Time:</strong> 5:30-7:00 AM in May-June (water-filled paddies) or late September (golden rice ready for harvest). <strong>Lens:</strong> Wide angle for the sweeping terraces, telephoto for abstract pattern details. Stay overnight in Ping'an to be at the viewpoint for sunrise.</p>

  <h2>Xianggong Mountain</h2>
  <p>About 30 minutes by car from Yangshuo, then a 20-minute hike. This is the classic elevated view of the Li River bend looking toward Xingping — layers of karst peaks fading into the distance. <strong>Time:</strong> Sunrise. <strong>Lens:</strong> Wide angle. Less crowded than Laozhai Mountain but growing in popularity.</p>

  <h2>Ten Mile Gallery Countryside</h2>
  <p>Not a single spot — the entire road is photogenic. The best approach: bike slowly and stop whenever the light hits the peaks in a way that makes you reach for your camera. <strong>Best light:</strong> 7-9 AM (morning mist and golden side-light on the peaks) and 4-6 PM (warm light). <strong>Lens:</strong> 24-70mm walkaround. A 70-200mm for isolating individual peaks with rice paddies in the foreground.</p>

  <h2>Elephant Trunk Hill at Sunset, Guilin</h2>
  <p>The city icon backlit by the setting sun. Shoot from the riverside path on the west bank of the Li River for the best angle. <strong>Time:</strong> 30 minutes before sunset. <strong>Lens:</strong> 24-70mm.</p>

  <div class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-50 p-6">
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-stone-400">Gear Tips for Guilin</p>
    <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-700">
      <li><strong>Tripod:</strong> Essential for sunrise and misty mornings. The best light happens when it's still dim.</li>
      <li><strong>Polarizing filter:</strong> Cuts reflections off water and wet foliage. Especially useful on the Li River.</li>
      <li><strong>Weather sealing:</strong> Guilin gets a lot of rain. Bring a rain cover for your camera and a microfiber cloth.</li>
      <li><strong>Drone:</strong> Stunning aerial potential, but China's drone regulations are strict. Registration is required for drones over 250g. Many scenic areas have no-fly zones. Check local rules before flying.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

